1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,080
When a light moves in a game, why can brightness remain on a wall
while a shadow follows the change?

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:11,220
We will connect DOOM’s sector brightness to Quake’s lightmaps,

3
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:15,280
real-time shadows, and the ordering of forward and deferred
shading.

4
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:22,820
You will be able to distinguish stored data from per-frame work and
identify where an image’s limitations come from.

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,720
Let us begin with a single surface.

6
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:29,860
A game image consists of small color cells called pixels.

7
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:35,100
Filling them produces one frame, and displaying successive frames
creates motion.

8
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:39,900
Rendering turns the scene into an image.

9
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:44,100
Start by asking what should appear at one position in this view.

10
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:50,477
Making a pixel starts with identifying the visible surface.

11
00:00:50,757 --> 00:00:55,457
The next task is estimating how much light that surface sends
toward the camera.

12
00:01:04,567 --> 00:01:08,267
A 3D object’s surface is divided into small triangles.

13
00:01:08,507 --> 00:01:14,527
Rasterization projects them onto the flat image and finds the
samples they cover.

14
00:01:14,807 --> 00:01:18,547
A depth test helps select the surface in front when surfaces overlap.

15
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:20,740
Follow the surfaces visible to the camera.

16
00:01:20,740 --> 00:01:28,020
Objects are three-dimensional, but the image is flat, so front and
back surfaces can overlap at one image location.

17
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,960
Depth distinguishes their order.

18
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:34,520
A normal instead describes surface orientation.

19
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:39,160
Position, depth and direction are different inputs to shading.

20
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:02,910
Visibility alone does not calculate lighting.

21
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:07,990
Position, surface normal, material, and light data are needed to
determine color.

22
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:11,830
The normal describes a surface’s orientation.

23
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:16,530
Imagine illuminating paper head-on, then at a slant.

24
00:02:16,670 --> 00:02:19,930
A dot product measures how closely the directions align.

25
00:02:20,290 --> 00:02:25,730
For two arrows normalized to length one, alignment gives one and a
sixty-degree angle gives 0.5.

26
00:02:26,090 --> 00:02:31,170
In this simple model, with other inputs fixed, the directional
contribution is halved.

27
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,580
A vector is an arrow with a direction and length.

28
00:02:34,840 --> 00:02:39,980
A unit vector has length one, allowing us to compare direction
without mixing in length.

29
00:02:40,260 --> 00:02:45,260
Good alignment does not guarantee illumination: a wall in between
can block the light.

30
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:47,580
Directional response and visibility are separate tests.

31
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,500
The long equation is a ledger of light.

32
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:54,160
It adds emitted light to light arriving from elsewhere and reflected
toward us.

33
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,780
The material determines how much is reflected.

34
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:01,500
The integral indicates that contributions from many directions are
combined.

35
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:03,333
You do not need to memorize the equation.

36
00:03:03,493 --> 00:03:10,693
Follow three questions: which surface is visible, can light reach it,
and how does that surface reflect it?

37
00:03:10,893 --> 00:03:15,793
Games may recompute these answers or store some of them in
advance.

38
00:03:17,667 --> 00:03:22,167
Switching to a reflection diagnostic changes how the same surfaces
appear.

39
00:03:22,547 --> 00:03:25,167
Distinguish the final game view from a diagnostic view.

40
00:03:27,533 --> 00:03:30,973
A ray is a test line extending from a point in a direction.

41
00:03:31,253 --> 00:03:37,073
From the camera it can find the first visible object; from a surface
toward a light it can test for blockers.

42
00:03:37,413 --> 00:03:41,353
A reflected ray can find an object visible in a mirror.

43
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:44,950
A rasterized image can still use ray-based calculations.

44
00:03:45,290 --> 00:03:50,130
Rays may compute lighting before gameplay, while the running
game reads the stored result.

45
00:03:52,383 --> 00:03:58,539
Our first choice replaces complex light transport with a brightness
value assigned to each part of the world.

46
00:03:59,990 --> 00:04:05,130
DOOM’s basic 1993 lighting starts with a sector brightness value.

47
00:04:05,390 --> 00:04:11,650
The map assigns brightness to a sector, and the renderer uses it to
choose a color table.

48
00:04:14,383 --> 00:04:19,843
A colormap provides colors that display the same wall pattern at
different brightnesses.

49
00:04:20,103 --> 00:04:25,303
The code’s bit shift groups sector brightness into coarse levels to
form an index.

50
00:04:25,603 --> 00:04:31,723
Extra lighting and orientation adjustments are applied, with the
index restricted to its valid range.

51
00:04:31,753 --> 00:04:35,733
Horizontal and vertical walls receive different adjustments.

52
00:04:35,993 --> 00:04:41,693
This is an axis-based rule, rather than a continuous calculation of the
angle to a light.

53
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:46,393
Two perpendicular walls can appear at different brightness in the
same sector.

54
00:04:46,553 --> 00:04:51,113
The sector has not changed; the wall orientation changes the
color-table selection.

55
00:04:51,433 --> 00:04:55,413
This is a simple orientation distinction rather than continuous
lighting over a curved surface.

56
00:04:55,636 --> 00:05:00,376
Distance-related behavior also enters through scale-dependent
color-table selection.

57
00:05:00,716 --> 00:05:04,676
Reducing the process to a single sector lookup would omit that
stage.

58
00:05:04,956 --> 00:05:08,576
This is not a per-light visibility test between an object and a lamp.

59
00:05:08,836 --> 00:05:14,356
The scheme alone does not automatically produce a local light
footprint or a precise object shadow.

60
00:05:14,656 --> 00:05:17,756
The footage is labeled as the 2024 rerelease.

61
00:05:17,756 --> 00:05:24,056
Later lighting added by a modern source port is not evidence of the
1993 implementation, even on the same DOOM map.

62
00:05:24,276 --> 00:05:29,656
We inspect the original sector-brightness and wall-orientation rules
separately in released source and our diagram.

63
00:05:30,633 --> 00:05:33,833
A texture can be a digital picture applied to a surface, such as
brickwork.

64
00:05:34,253 --> 00:05:39,153
Quake stores another image describing illumination at different
places on that wall.

65
00:05:39,633 --> 00:05:41,613
This lighting image is the lightmap.

66
00:05:41,893 --> 00:05:44,993
Separate the pattern picture from the light picture.

67
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,900
As the viewpoint moves, the pattern stays attached to the surface.

68
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:52,760
Keep its texture coordinates distinct from the coordinates used to
store lighting.

69
00:05:54,033 --> 00:05:59,333
Instead of computing every point on a continuous wall, we choose
representative samples.

70
00:05:59,793 --> 00:06:05,333
At each sample we test visibility to the light and account for
direction, distance and intensity.

71
00:06:05,653 --> 00:06:09,253
Computing these results before gameplay is called baking.

72
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:13,517
Our diagram fills samples in sequence and transfers the results to a
texture.

73
00:06:13,837 --> 00:06:17,657
Samples behind the box are darker because the light is blocked.

74
00:06:18,050 --> 00:06:21,850
Separate the repeating wall pattern from the broad illumination in
these rooms.

75
00:06:22,110 --> 00:06:25,530
Changing the pattern does not automatically recompute lighting.

76
00:06:25,730 --> 00:06:31,030
Increasing lightmap resolution does not add detail to the original
brick picture either.

77
00:06:31,290 --> 00:06:32,850
The two data sources serve different purposes.

78
00:06:39,050 --> 00:06:43,490
In this example, the seams between wall pieces stand out.

79
00:06:43,790 --> 00:06:47,670
Check whether stored lighting joins smoothly across neighboring
surfaces.

80
00:06:47,890 --> 00:06:51,010
The visible seam alone does not establish a single cause.

81
00:06:58,917 --> 00:07:02,257
A wall texture may contain fine detail while its lightmap uses coarse
samples.

82
00:07:02,497 --> 00:07:06,277
Raising texture resolution alone does not refine the lighting
boundary.

83
00:07:06,477 --> 00:07:11,477
Raising lightmap resolution improves stored lighting detail without
inventing detail in the wall texture.

84
00:07:11,683 --> 00:07:14,437
A later engine demonstration makes the distinction observable.

85
00:07:14,437 --> 00:07:21,817
The 2017 Unreal comparison rooms are labeled with lightmap
resolutions of 128, 256, 512, and 1,024.

86
00:07:22,077 --> 00:07:24,257
Separate texture detail from lighting boundaries.

87
00:07:24,557 --> 00:07:30,097
The camera visits different authored rooms; this is not a
single-object on/off experiment.

88
00:07:30,467 --> 00:07:33,907
The numbers in each room identify the displayed comparison
conditions.

89
00:07:34,207 --> 00:07:38,427
First inspect the wall pattern, then the boundary where a shadow
crosses it.

90
00:07:38,707 --> 00:07:43,407
The two kinds of detail need not be stored at the same density.

91
00:07:45,683 --> 00:07:51,023
At runtime, the renderer reads the wall pattern and the lighting
value for the corresponding surface location, then combines them.

92
00:07:51,323 --> 00:07:54,703
Texture coordinates specify where to look in an image.

93
00:07:55,023 --> 00:07:57,863
The two images can use different layouts and resolutions.

94
00:07:58,067 --> 00:08:02,527
Inspect texture sharpness separately from lighting-edge sharpness.

95
00:08:08,683 --> 00:08:11,523
Direct light travels from a light to a surface.

96
00:08:11,823 --> 00:08:14,343
Indirect light arrives after reflecting off another surface.

97
00:08:14,563 --> 00:08:19,443
Quake II’s tools also computed reflected contributions between
surface patches and added them to lightmap samples,

98
00:08:19,743 --> 00:08:22,663
illuminating places without direct exposure.

99
00:08:22,817 --> 00:08:25,337
Even a dark passage need not have a uniform color.

100
00:08:25,577 --> 00:08:30,157
Its appearance alone does not identify the algorithm used to
compute indirect lighting.

101
00:08:30,183 --> 00:08:34,037
This should not be labeled photon mapping.

102
00:08:34,317 --> 00:08:38,757
Algorithms can share the goal of storing indirect light while
calculating it through different processes.

103
00:08:38,967 --> 00:08:43,427
A similar visual impression and an identical calculation method are
different claims.

104
00:08:45,183 --> 00:08:48,539
Thinking in separate layers explains update cost.

105
00:08:48,739 --> 00:08:52,599
Changing a wall texture and moving a light are different edits.

106
00:08:52,899 --> 00:08:55,159
The latter changes which samples receive illumination,

107
00:08:55,259 --> 00:08:59,019
so stored visibility and indirect lighting must be checked against the
new scene.

108
00:08:59,350 --> 00:09:06,750
Changing a wall’s color changes a material input; moving a light
changes light paths.

109
00:09:07,010 --> 00:09:09,830
This view lets us inspect the room’s structure and illumination.

110
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:15,490
Which information needs rebuilding depends on which input was
changed.

111
00:09:24,186 --> 00:09:26,146
Moving a light exposes the limitation:

112
00:09:26,226 --> 00:09:28,426
stored values describe the old configuration.

113
00:09:28,546 --> 00:09:33,146
New visibility and orientation require recalculation or an additional
dynamic-lighting method.

114
00:09:33,583 --> 00:09:39,643
This is object manipulation in the editor, not a completed light-off
and rebake comparison.

115
00:09:42,187 --> 00:09:45,267
A moving character is not attached to one wall location.

116
00:09:45,267 --> 00:09:50,087
Quake includes a downward query that samples surface lighting.

117
00:09:50,227 --> 00:09:53,687
Static surface storage and a moving object’s lighting query are
separate concerns.

118
00:09:54,567 --> 00:10:00,347
Moving the camera does not make stored lighting samples follow it.

119
00:10:00,667 --> 00:10:04,167
A moving object must retrieve values appropriate to its own
location.

120
00:10:04,367 --> 00:10:08,827
Shortly we will compare samples on a wall with samples distributed
through space.

121
00:10:15,433 --> 00:10:19,413
A pixel belongs to the output image; a texel belongs to a texture.

122
00:10:19,733 --> 00:10:21,333
They are not necessarily one-to-one.

123
00:10:21,493 --> 00:10:24,793
A distant floor can put many texels inside one pixel’s footprint.

124
00:10:25,033 --> 00:10:30,073
Choosing just one can make the pattern fluctuate as the camera
moves.

125
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:35,610
Viewed obliquely, distant road details crowd into a narrow part of
the image.

126
00:10:35,870 --> 00:10:41,630
Selecting one texel as if it were a nearby surface cannot adequately
represent that larger region.

127
00:10:49,433 --> 00:10:52,793
Mipmaps store progressively smaller versions of a texture.

128
00:10:52,873 --> 00:10:58,833
Selecting an appropriate level avoids repeatedly resolving details
finer than the pixel’s footprint.

129
00:10:59,083 --> 00:11:02,063
Smaller mip levels combine fine details.

130
00:11:02,163 --> 00:11:07,703
The purpose is to choose values representing the sampled region,
not to select a blurry image arbitrarily.

131
00:11:08,233 --> 00:11:11,353
When a value is needed between texture samples, bilinear filtering
combines four neighbors.

132
00:11:11,853 --> 00:11:15,773
Their weights determine how much each contributes, with closer
samples contributing more.

133
00:11:16,173 --> 00:11:19,873
This operation works within one texture level.

134
00:11:20,090 --> 00:11:23,710
Take a horizontal fraction of one quarter and a vertical fraction of
one half.

135
00:11:23,710 --> 00:11:29,590
The top-left and bottom-left weights are each three eighths; the
right-hand weights are each one eighth.

136
00:11:29,590 --> 00:11:33,950
They sum to one, and the nearer left values contribute more.

137
00:11:34,183 --> 00:11:37,363
A texture can hold data other than photographic color.

138
00:11:37,543 --> 00:11:40,963
This view separates masks packed into different channels.

139
00:11:41,203 --> 00:11:44,863
Red, green and blue do not automatically identify a normal map.

140
00:11:45,123 --> 00:11:49,383
Before interpreting filtering, identify what the blended values
actually mean.

141
00:11:53,250 --> 00:11:58,110
As distance increases, a smaller mip level may be appropriate, but
abrupt switches can be visible.

142
00:11:58,170 --> 00:12:05,790
Trilinear filtering blends the bilinear results from two neighboring
mip levels: interpolation within each level and between levels.

143
00:12:05,819 --> 00:12:09,399
At an oblique viewing angle, a pixel’s footprint becomes elongated.

144
00:12:09,519 --> 00:12:15,299
Anisotropic filtering accounts for this directional shape instead of
using only a roughly square footprint.

145
00:12:15,533 --> 00:12:18,193
Look at the thin stair edges at an angle.

146
00:12:18,473 --> 00:12:22,133
One pixel can contain both an edge and empty space.

147
00:12:22,193 --> 00:12:30,013
Testing one location may miss that edge; testing coverage at several
locations can represent it more consistently.

148
00:12:30,193 --> 00:12:33,153
This motivates multisampling later.

149
00:12:33,253 --> 00:12:37,113
Texture filtering instead combines values from a surface texture.

150
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:04,699
Filtering does not invent missing texture detail.

151
00:13:04,859 --> 00:13:10,979
It reads existing data more appropriately for screen samples, with
costs that depend on the chosen quality.

152
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:19,453
Independent of the lighting model, processing order matters.

153
00:13:19,693 --> 00:13:25,133
Tile-based rendering divides the screen into smaller regions and
assigns triangles to them.

154
00:13:25,483 --> 00:13:29,503
Tilting the camera toward the building changes the geometry
covering the image.

155
00:13:29,763 --> 00:13:35,143
Classifying those triangles by the image region they affect is the
starting point for our tile explanation.

156
00:13:35,583 --> 00:13:39,943
This engine view is not an execution trace of historical PowerVR
hardware.

157
00:13:44,053 --> 00:13:48,193
The 1990s PowerVR lineage illustrates this hardware approach.

158
00:13:48,313 --> 00:13:53,553
We will not project the complete structure of a modern
programmable renderer onto those earlier devices.

159
00:13:53,917 --> 00:13:58,557
Repeatedly moving data to and from external memory costs
bandwidth.

160
00:13:58,777 --> 00:14:04,997
Keeping a small tile’s working data in nearby on-chip storage can
reduce those trips.

161
00:14:05,217 --> 00:14:07,477
The tile is completed and its result is then written out.

162
00:14:07,517 --> 00:14:10,837
Shading computes a surface’s color and illumination.

163
00:14:10,997 --> 00:14:16,217
PowerVR’s TBDR resolves visibility before performing needed
shading.

164
00:14:16,477 --> 00:14:18,837
Deferred means postponed.

165
00:14:19,197 --> 00:14:22,297
The later technique called deferred shading postpones a different
part of the work.

166
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:24,426
The list here contains primitives.

167
00:14:24,506 --> 00:14:29,326
Tiled lighting instead lists lights affecting a screen region.

168
00:14:29,386 --> 00:14:32,566
Similar-looking grids can contain different data for different
purposes.

169
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:36,040
A surface fully hidden behind an opaque wall may not need shading.

170
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:40,600
Transparent or discarding materials require different handling.

171
00:14:41,180 --> 00:14:44,800
Other GPUs also test depth early and reuse nearby data.

172
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,200
The word tile alone does not determine performance.

173
00:14:48,453 --> 00:14:55,573
DOOM 3’s 2004 approach calculates direct-light and shadow
responses to moving lights and objects.

174
00:14:55,833 --> 00:14:59,273
This is a different update task from reading baked indirect lighting.

175
00:14:59,783 --> 00:15:02,703
Inspect the lit surfaces from the moving viewpoint.

176
00:15:04,453 --> 00:15:10,393
A simple diffuse model receives more direct light when its normal
faces the light.

177
00:15:10,693 --> 00:15:14,433
That example does not describe every material or effect in the game.

178
00:15:15,950 --> 00:15:18,730
A normal map stores small-scale surface orientation in a texture.

179
00:15:18,730 --> 00:15:25,810
It can produce rock-like shading without automatically changing a
flat surface’s outer silhouette.

180
00:15:25,817 --> 00:15:32,290
Official DOOM 3 game footage moves through dark industrial spaces
and shows illuminated characters and structures.

181
00:15:32,710 --> 00:15:39,010
We first observe that appearance, then examine shadow
construction through released source and our diagram.

182
00:15:39,010 --> 00:15:42,590
Game pixels alone do not reveal every stencil operation.

183
00:15:42,917 --> 00:15:46,977
While moving, distinguish surface brightness from the object’s
silhouette.

184
00:15:47,237 --> 00:15:50,017
The next diagram constructs the space behind that boundary.

185
00:15:50,817 --> 00:15:55,057
Put a hand in front of a flashlight and a region behind it loses that
light.

186
00:15:55,297 --> 00:15:58,497
Shadow volumes construct this region in 3D.

187
00:15:58,857 --> 00:16:06,637
Edges on the light-facing silhouette are extruded and connected into
side faces, as seen in DOOM 3’s public code.

188
00:16:06,667 --> 00:16:10,067
We test whether the visible surface point lies inside that region.

189
00:16:10,567 --> 00:16:14,207
The stencil buffer stores test values at image locations.

190
00:16:14,627 --> 00:16:20,267
If a point is in shadow, that light’s contribution is omitted; this is not
simply a black image pasted onto the scene.

191
00:16:20,750 --> 00:16:24,790
The stencil test is not simply painting the volume sides black.

192
00:16:25,050 --> 00:16:29,550
It counts how the test path crosses front and back faces

193
00:16:29,550 --> 00:16:34,550
to determine whether a sample is inside the shadowed region.

194
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:39,530
Camera-inside cases also require careful counting direction and cap
handling.

195
00:16:39,650 --> 00:16:44,750
A point-light assumption produces sharp boundaries, and large
screen coverage can increase cost.

196
00:16:44,910 --> 00:16:49,830
Real-time direct lighting does not by itself solve indirect reflections
or color bleeding.

197
00:16:50,700 --> 00:16:54,240
Separate the robot’s interior shading from its silhouette.

198
00:16:54,400 --> 00:17:03,680
Changing the directions used for lighting can suggest grooves, but it
does not create the actual protruding shape visible in silhouette.

199
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:09,240
Normal mapping changes shading directions, not the underlying
geometry.

200
00:17:21,650 --> 00:17:27,223
Half-Life 2 illustrates another 2004 choice: richer use of baked
lighting.

201
00:17:27,583 --> 00:17:32,223
Responding to normal-map orientation requires more information
than one brightness value.

202
00:17:32,733 --> 00:17:36,533
Notice the impression that the character and background occupy
one space.

203
00:17:36,773 --> 00:17:42,093
That does not mean static walls and moving characters must
retrieve lighting from the same storage.

204
00:17:42,453 --> 00:17:46,033
We will separate directional components from spatial samples.

205
00:17:48,650 --> 00:17:52,530
One brightness value loses the direction from which light arrived.

206
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:58,790
Valve’s example stores lighting for three reference directions, called
a basis.

207
00:17:59,050 --> 00:18:03,990
The three arrows are not three newly added lights; they define
components of stored directional information.

208
00:18:04,017 --> 00:18:09,297
Determine how closely the surface faces each basis direction and
blend the stored colors using those weights.

209
00:18:09,737 --> 00:18:13,557
Multiplication computes each contribution; addition combines them.

210
00:18:14,017 --> 00:18:19,037
This source’s clamped dot-product example is distinct from
squared-weight formulations in other versions.

211
00:18:19,350 --> 00:18:22,930
The final image does not display directional lighting components
separately.

212
00:18:23,390 --> 00:18:28,770
The game shows the result; the diagram separates the intermediate
values producing it.

213
00:18:29,190 --> 00:18:32,570
Reading them together makes the storage scheme meaningful.

214
00:18:36,217 --> 00:18:38,753
Suppose the three stored directional colors differ.

215
00:18:39,033 --> 00:18:42,833
A normal facing the first basis increases that lightmap’s
contribution;

216
00:18:42,833 --> 00:18:46,033
rotating the normal shifts the weights toward the other directions.

217
00:18:46,293 --> 00:18:51,513
Stored colors stay fixed while their contributions change, revealing
small surface detail.

218
00:18:52,200 --> 00:18:54,780
A weight is a mixing proportion.

219
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:58,760
Even with the same ingredients, changing their proportions changes
the result.

220
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:04,300
Directional lightmaps give more weight to components aligned with
the surface.

221
00:19:04,560 --> 00:19:07,320
Here the robot is inspected from different viewpoints.

222
00:19:07,540 --> 00:19:11,320
Distinguish surface orientation from camera motion.

223
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:15,020
Materials and lighting also affect the real image.

224
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:21,380
Our diagram isolates surface direction so stored values and the
weights used to read them can be considered separately.

225
00:19:54,073 --> 00:19:56,853
Changing the normal changes the directional contributions.

226
00:19:57,053 --> 00:20:03,013
This adds small-scale orientation response beyond one flat
brightness value,

227
00:20:03,313 --> 00:20:07,313
without reconstructing geometry or every dynamic occlusion.

228
00:20:07,733 --> 00:20:12,173
A single combined value loses directional information.

229
00:20:12,393 --> 00:20:17,073
Keeping separate directional components allows a surface to select
more of the contribution it faces.

230
00:20:17,273 --> 00:20:19,473
This does not independently generate new moving shadows.

231
00:20:19,873 --> 00:20:25,873
The game shows completed shading; the diagram separates the
directional contributions.

232
00:20:44,600 --> 00:20:49,993
Our spatial diagram separates a rock-like surface into actual
geometry and normal-map detail.

233
00:20:50,253 --> 00:20:55,593
Changing normal arrows affects internal shading while the outer
silhouette remains flat.

234
00:20:55,773 --> 00:20:59,313
Moving real vertices also changes silhouette and occlusion.

235
00:20:59,593 --> 00:21:02,353
The distinction is the information each representation creates.

236
00:21:03,133 --> 00:21:05,533
Here we inspect the robot’s arm and torso from different directions.

237
00:21:05,533 --> 00:21:14,073
The outline gives clues about geometry, while small variations inside
a face give clues about material and orientation.

238
00:21:14,593 --> 00:21:18,473
Treating them as the same thing overstates what normal maps can
do.

239
00:21:18,973 --> 00:21:22,573
Our earlier diagram changed directions without moving the plane’s
vertices.

240
00:21:23,233 --> 00:21:26,573
Apply the same habit of separating outline from interior detail here.

241
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:44,760
A wall lightmap stays attached to the wall, but a character moves.

242
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:50,020
Lighting can instead be prepared at locations in the room and
nearby information retrieved.

243
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:53,180
Such spatial samples are commonly called probes.

244
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:58,020
The historical ambient cube and other engines’ probe systems use
different representations.

245
00:21:58,350 --> 00:22:03,410
Think of these points as locations storing lighting information, not
little light sources.

246
00:22:03,690 --> 00:22:07,970
A wall texture stays on a surface; these samples occupy volume,
including height.

247
00:22:08,150 --> 00:22:11,710
A moving character uses nearby information.

248
00:22:11,990 --> 00:22:18,050
Interpolation combines values between samples to avoid abrupt
changes.

249
00:22:18,210 --> 00:22:21,290
This Unreal example is a different engine from Half-Life 2.

250
00:22:33,133 --> 00:22:37,393
As the camera turns into the room, walls and spatial points appear
together.

251
00:22:37,613 --> 00:22:41,433
Distinguish the lighting image attached to a wall from lighting
information sampled in space.

252
00:22:41,713 --> 00:22:44,233
The points are not light-emitting bulbs.

253
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:56,873
A later Unreal lighting demonstration displays spatial samples and
moves a character between a dark room and a bright floor.

254
00:22:57,193 --> 00:23:01,693
A surface-texel grid and samples distributed through space store
information in different locations.

255
00:23:01,733 --> 00:23:08,813
This is a 2017 engine example of spatial lighting, not the identical
implementation of Half-Life 2 ambient cubes.

256
00:23:09,383 --> 00:23:13,043
First inspect the character together with the surrounding sample
visualization.

257
00:23:13,523 --> 00:23:19,843
Locate the prepared values, then follow the character as it changes
position.

258
00:23:20,243 --> 00:23:27,403
It cannot carry a single wall texel around the room; it needs lighting
information appropriate to its location.

259
00:23:27,743 --> 00:23:32,263
The room interior and the floor outside have different surroundings
and incoming illumination.

260
00:23:32,783 --> 00:23:38,223
As the character moves between them, inspect its body brightness
together with its position.

261
00:23:38,283 --> 00:23:45,443
The displayed samples are spatial clues for this lookup, not
necessarily actual light sources.

262
00:23:45,783 --> 00:23:49,223
Later, a changed viewpoint reveals the samples and object from
another direction.

263
00:23:49,483 --> 00:23:54,043
Two points close in the image may be separated in depth.

264
00:23:54,363 --> 00:23:58,603
Unlike a surface lightmap, this distribution must be read in height as
well.

265
00:23:58,923 --> 00:24:04,723
These operations do not measure exact interpolation weights or
per-frame cost.

266
00:24:04,983 --> 00:24:07,743
Internal values require separate engine evidence.

267
00:24:42,133 --> 00:24:44,633
Finally inspect the room from another viewpoint.

268
00:24:44,773 --> 00:24:49,493
Consider sample placement together with the places a character can
occupy.

269
00:24:49,753 --> 00:24:53,813
The existence of samples does not make lighting exact everywhere.

270
00:24:54,113 --> 00:24:58,793
Their density, placement and interpolation are further design
choices.

271
00:24:59,333 --> 00:25:03,953
The key here is that storage is in space rather than on the wall.

272
00:25:14,003 --> 00:25:17,503
Higher-quality baking consumes preparation time and storage.

273
00:25:17,923 --> 00:25:22,283
Lower runtime cost trades against production work and update cost.

274
00:25:22,683 --> 00:25:26,143
One map’s bake time cannot serve as a universal figure for games.

275
00:25:26,650 --> 00:25:29,790
Precomputation lets the game read prepared answers.

276
00:25:30,090 --> 00:25:34,190
Changing the room or lighting may invalidate them.

277
00:25:34,450 --> 00:25:40,390
This editor view inspects a scene; its playback time is not a
measurement of baking time.

278
00:25:51,007 --> 00:25:55,407
A remaining baked contribution after a light changes follows from
this assumption.

279
00:25:55,787 --> 00:26:00,047
Hybrid systems may update direct light dynamically while retaining
static indirect light,

280
00:26:00,427 --> 00:26:02,847
so the two update schedules must be examined separately.

281
00:26:03,700 --> 00:26:05,720
Imagine a camera at the light.

282
00:26:06,080 --> 00:26:11,540
Instead of colors, it records depth to the first surface in each image
direction: a shadow map.

283
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:16,960
Projecting a visible surface point into that light view lets us check
whether it lies behind the first recorded surface.

284
00:26:17,150 --> 00:26:20,510
In this example, smaller depth means nearer and larger means
farther.

285
00:26:20,690 --> 00:26:24,630
A test point behind the stored surface is blocked.

286
00:26:24,930 --> 00:26:30,890
Just as rulers need matching origins and units, both depths must be
compared using the same light-view convention.

287
00:26:31,050 --> 00:26:33,950
Disabling a light differs from disabling shadows.

288
00:26:35,917 --> 00:26:39,337
Removing the whole light contribution first darkens the terrain.

289
00:26:42,917 --> 00:26:45,497
After restoring it, shadow settings are changed.

290
00:26:50,917 --> 00:26:56,537
Distinguish shading caused by surface orientation from shadows
cast when another surface blocks light.

291
00:27:06,917 --> 00:27:13,857
For a numerical example, stored depth of 0.33 and receiver depth of
0.80

292
00:27:13,857 --> 00:27:16,537
place the receiver behind the first surface.

293
00:27:16,537 --> 00:27:23,877
If moving the blocker changes stored depth to 0.90, the receiver at
0.80 lies in front and is unoccluded by this test.

294
00:27:23,883 --> 00:27:27,157
Magnifying the light’s depth image reveals its texel grid.

295
00:27:27,157 --> 00:27:34,277
Covering a large region with few texels can create stair-stepped
shadow edges, even when the original shape is smooth.

296
00:27:34,550 --> 00:27:37,670
Stored numbers have limited precision.

297
00:27:37,870 --> 00:27:42,810
Two computations of the same surface depth can differ slightly,
making it appear to block itself.

298
00:27:43,070 --> 00:27:45,730
Bias adds a small adjustment to the comparison to reduce this
self-shadowing error.

299
00:27:45,750 --> 00:27:49,383
Excessive bias can detach the shadow from the object.

300
00:27:49,683 --> 00:27:54,123
A single chosen value is not guaranteed to solve every distance and
surface slope.

301
00:27:54,617 --> 00:27:57,557
A single shadow-map lookup can give a hard jagged boundary.

302
00:27:57,797 --> 00:28:03,317
PCF tests several nearby locations and averages the lit results.

303
00:28:03,577 --> 00:28:07,477
It combines comparison outcomes, rather than averaging depths
before one comparison.

304
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:12,996
If two of four neighboring tests report visibility, their PCF average is
one half.

305
00:28:12,996 --> 00:28:19,136
This produces an intermediate visibility value, but does not exactly
integrate every path from an area light.

306
00:28:19,336 --> 00:28:23,256
Wider filters require more samples and introduce further tradeoffs.

307
00:28:23,450 --> 00:28:26,170
A filter’s extent controls the neighborhood combined.

308
00:28:26,170 --> 00:28:31,570
PCSS adjusts it using the relationship between blockers and
receivers.

309
00:28:31,710 --> 00:28:36,210
Variance shadow maps store depth’s first and second moments to
approximate visibility.

310
00:28:36,510 --> 00:28:39,190
These are different approximations, with limitations such as light
leaking in the latter.

311
00:28:39,217 --> 00:28:44,093
Variance Shadow Maps and Unreal’s Virtual Shadow Maps share an
abbreviation but are different methods.

312
00:28:44,273 --> 00:28:47,933
A later episode will distinguish virtual-page shadow management.

313
00:28:48,817 --> 00:28:53,070
One map covering a broad outdoor scene can make nearby shadows
coarse.

314
00:28:53,330 --> 00:28:58,810
Cascaded Shadow Maps split the camera’s view by distance and
assign shadow maps to those regions.

315
00:28:59,070 --> 00:29:01,110
This does not create more lights.

316
00:29:01,430 --> 00:29:07,250
It records shadows from one directional light at different spatial
densities near and far from the camera.

317
00:29:07,590 --> 00:29:12,950
For an illustrative example, 2,048 texels covering 20 meters gives
roughly one centimeter per texel.

318
00:29:13,170 --> 00:29:16,010
Covering 200 meters with the same count gives roughly ten
centimeters.

319
00:29:16,490 --> 00:29:19,310
Those numbers are not settings or performance measurements
from a game.

320
00:29:19,750 --> 00:29:23,750
They illustrate how coverage changes world-space texel size at the
same resolution.

321
00:29:24,217 --> 00:29:31,617
Choose the shadow map for the point’s camera-distance interval
and blend results near transitions.

322
00:29:31,877 --> 00:29:39,077
Tiny camera motions can make the projected grid slide and shadows
shimmer, so stabilization can snap the projection to the texel grid.

323
00:29:39,103 --> 00:29:44,643
In the acquired official Unreal example, the character casts a visible
shadow while running on the floor, but not on the stairs.

324
00:29:44,983 --> 00:29:48,823
The documentation describes disabled CSM reception on the stairs.

325
00:29:49,050 --> 00:29:54,463
That clip demonstrates receiver configuration, rather than
visualizing cascade partitions.

326
00:29:54,823 --> 00:30:00,023
A separate spatial diagram explains the splits, while the observed
engine result illustrates the setting.

327
00:30:00,186 --> 00:30:04,206
A 2017 official Unreal demonstration shows the partitions more
directly.

328
00:30:04,406 --> 00:30:10,166
Enabling Show, Advanced, Shadow Frustums displays differently
colored projected boundaries over the scene.

329
00:30:10,446 --> 00:30:14,326
Observe how those boundaries change with the shadow distance
and distribution controls.

330
00:30:14,817 --> 00:30:19,357
The colored boundaries visualize shadow-recording regions, not
additional lights.

331
00:30:22,317 --> 00:30:27,257
Pulling the camera back reveals the spatial separation between the
nearby objects and distant terrain.

332
00:30:30,269 --> 00:30:36,629
The same demonstration changes the cascade count through four,
zero, one, and three while also adjusting shadow distance.

333
00:30:36,789 --> 00:30:40,629
Distinguish disabling cascades from assigning a broad region to one
map.

334
00:30:40,629 --> 00:30:46,249
Relate the one-versus-multiple edge comparison to our
equal-resolution coverage diagram.

335
00:30:46,389 --> 00:30:50,689
Several controls change, so we do not attribute every difference to
one parameter.

336
00:30:51,017 --> 00:30:53,617
Inspect near and far regions together.

337
00:30:53,897 --> 00:30:57,017
The total distance and distribution change as well as the number of
splits.

338
00:30:57,277 --> 00:31:04,837
Connect each visible setting change with the affected region instead
of drawing a conclusion from one still image.

339
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:11,260
After the nearby region, inspect distant terrain too.

340
00:31:11,580 --> 00:31:15,020
These settings control distant shadow coverage and cascade count.

341
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:18,900
Read the settings together with the region of the view they cover.

342
00:31:25,273 --> 00:31:28,213
More cascades require additional maps and work.

343
00:31:28,433 --> 00:31:32,893
The technique allocates a limited budget while managing transitions
and instability.

344
00:31:33,650 --> 00:31:37,270
Now compare work order for the same image.

345
00:31:37,550 --> 00:31:42,370
Forward shading evaluates the material and relevant lights while
drawing a surface.

346
00:31:42,690 --> 00:31:46,990
When processing this wall pixel, it computes the lighting
contribution as part of that surface’s shading.

347
00:31:47,017 --> 00:31:49,337
Deferred shading postpones lighting.

348
00:31:49,477 --> 00:31:54,277
It first records visible-surface properties such as base color,
orientation and depth by image position.

349
00:31:54,577 --> 00:31:57,077
This collection is the G-buffer.

350
00:31:57,357 --> 00:32:00,357
A later lighting stage reads those records to compute illumination.

351
00:32:00,517 --> 00:32:04,937
A buffer stores values for later processing.

352
00:32:05,157 --> 00:32:09,017
The G-buffer gathers properties of the visible surface at each image
location.

353
00:32:09,317 --> 00:32:14,677
Colors in this material view represent specific data rather than
decoration; their meaning must be identified.

354
00:32:14,897 --> 00:32:19,617
Connect surface information to lighting evaluation and then to the
final color.

355
00:32:26,517 --> 00:32:33,717
Unwrapping the book assigns image locations to its 3D faces: UV
coordinates.

356
00:32:34,097 --> 00:32:39,457
Base color describes material color, roughness the spread of
reflection, and normals the orientation used in lighting.

357
00:32:39,657 --> 00:32:42,797
One surface can have several property images.

358
00:32:43,157 --> 00:32:48,817
This is an object-material demonstration, distinct from a
screen-space G-buffer.

359
00:32:51,017 --> 00:32:54,157
Base color is not the final lit color.

360
00:32:54,477 --> 00:32:59,917
Normals encode orientation; roughness controls the spread of a
material’s reflection.

361
00:33:00,097 --> 00:33:05,157
The Unreal book example inspects and exports material data
separately.

362
00:33:05,377 --> 00:33:12,517
Object textures and the current frame’s G-buffer use different
domains, but the demonstration helps distinguish property types.

363
00:33:15,817 --> 00:33:18,177
Follow one pixel on the same wall.

364
00:33:18,357 --> 00:33:23,897
Suppose two lighting contributions, already accounting for the
material, are 0.25 and 0.35; their sum is 0.6.

365
00:33:24,037 --> 00:33:26,937
Forward computes this during surface shading.

366
00:33:27,137 --> 00:33:30,357
Deferred records surface data first, then combines lighting in a later
stage.

367
00:33:30,597 --> 00:33:35,637
The goal is the same; the ordering and intermediate data differ.

368
00:33:35,667 --> 00:33:40,187
If a light only reaches the right wall, pixels on the left need not
evaluate it.

369
00:33:40,787 --> 00:33:43,667
Both approaches can cull irrelevant lights.

370
00:33:44,027 --> 00:33:52,207
The four objects and three lights illustrate ordering, not a claim that
Forward always evaluates every light or Deferred always wins.

371
00:33:52,317 --> 00:33:57,130
S.T.A.L.K.E.R.’s development materials describe a deferred approach
for many lights.

372
00:33:57,450 --> 00:34:04,970
Its counts and performance figures belong to the documented scene
and hardware context.

373
00:34:05,350 --> 00:34:09,810
A record is useful to a later operation, but writing and reading it
costs work and bandwidth.

374
00:34:09,950 --> 00:34:11,330
The G-buffer has that tradeoff.

375
00:34:11,550 --> 00:34:19,650
A record of only one frontmost surface cannot by itself describe all
the layers needed for transparent glass.

376
00:34:19,683 --> 00:34:22,817
Place two glass layers in front of an opaque wall.

377
00:34:23,157 --> 00:34:29,857
A buffer storing only the nearest surface does not contain every
layer’s material.

378
00:34:29,857 --> 00:34:33,737
Compositing them needs additional information or passes.

379
00:34:33,737 --> 00:34:40,177
A real engine can therefore combine rendering paths within one
frame.

380
00:34:40,250 --> 00:34:43,530
A pass is a stage or round of rendering work.

381
00:34:43,650 --> 00:34:48,910
An engine can shade opaque objects with Deferred and composite
glass in a separate Forward pass.

382
00:34:49,170 --> 00:34:55,190
Light pre-pass is another compromise: lighting from depth and
normals, followed by material combination.

383
00:34:55,217 --> 00:34:57,057
Forward remains in use.

384
00:34:57,397 --> 00:35:01,077
Robo Recall is a VR game whose view follows head movement.

385
00:35:01,337 --> 00:35:07,937
Its Forward path was chosen with multisampling and required
features in mind.

386
00:35:08,137 --> 00:35:12,317
We use this demonstration to connect that engineering choice to
visible image quality.

387
00:35:16,650 --> 00:35:19,710
This is actual VR interaction with objects.

388
00:35:20,250 --> 00:35:22,550
Inspect both the response and the image edges.

389
00:35:22,717 --> 00:35:28,217
This game illustrates VR’s moving thin edges.

390
00:35:28,477 --> 00:35:31,677
Follow the outlines of hands, robots and fragments.

391
00:35:31,897 --> 00:35:38,217
Image quality depends on pixel coverage as well as the order of
lighting calculations.

392
00:35:38,497 --> 00:35:41,577
Next, inspect those edges in the magnified comparison.

393
00:35:48,650 --> 00:35:52,810
Anti-aliasing reduces jagged or unstable image edges.

394
00:35:53,110 --> 00:35:56,690
Multisampling tests coverage at several positions inside a pixel.

395
00:35:56,930 --> 00:36:00,930
If two of four positions are covered, the edge can contribute roughly
half the coverage;

396
00:36:01,330 --> 00:36:05,610
this is an illustrative example, not a claim about the game’s sample
setting.

397
00:36:05,790 --> 00:36:08,270
Observe the magnified line.

398
00:36:08,490 --> 00:36:13,850
Multiple coverage tests do not necessarily repeat every material and
lighting computation that many times.

399
00:36:14,110 --> 00:36:16,850
Cost depends on implementation and settings.

400
00:36:17,150 --> 00:36:21,870
Forward versus Deferred is therefore a choice involving image
quality, features and memory costs,

401
00:36:21,870 --> 00:36:23,070
not simply which name is newer.

402
00:36:50,520 --> 00:36:57,320
Missing screen-space features in a particular Unreal forward
implementation are implementation constraints,

403
00:36:57,720 --> 00:37:02,180
not proof that such effects are mathematically impossible in every
forward renderer.

404
00:37:02,700 --> 00:37:07,400
Changing diagnostic modes reveals information otherwise
combined in the final image.

405
00:37:07,660 --> 00:37:12,380
This reflection view is not automatically screen-space reflection.

406
00:37:12,640 --> 00:37:16,360
Even when effect names resemble one another, their inputs and
implementation must be identified.

407
00:37:22,517 --> 00:37:25,510
We also distinguish this from TBDR’s deferred terminology.

408
00:37:25,810 --> 00:37:32,190
That division concerns visibility before shading; this division
concerns material storage before lighting evaluation.

409
00:37:32,983 --> 00:37:37,443
Even after direct shadows, corners can appear ungrounded.

410
00:37:37,703 --> 00:37:43,143
A point in a crevice has fewer open directions for environmental
illumination than an exposed point.

411
00:37:43,403 --> 00:37:50,403
Screen-space ambient occlusion, SSAO, estimates this nearby
occlusion using recorded screen depth.

412
00:37:50,483 --> 00:37:55,503
Screen-space means operating on information available in the
current camera image.

413
00:37:55,503 --> 00:37:59,103
SSAO does not trace all lighting through the scene.

414
00:37:59,383 --> 00:38:03,983
It examines nearby screen-depth samples to estimate occlusion
around a surface.

415
00:38:04,024 --> 00:38:08,224
Our diagram places samples around a box corner and compares
depths.

416
00:38:08,464 --> 00:38:15,104
A small radius emphasizes contact; a larger one reaches farther but
can increase undersampling and incorrect associations.

417
00:38:15,304 --> 00:38:20,024
Filtering can reduce noise but may blur across boundaries.

418
00:38:20,084 --> 00:38:23,564
Sample count, filtering, and depth differences must be considered
together.

419
00:38:23,824 --> 00:38:27,224
The depth buffer primarily describes visible front surfaces.

420
00:38:27,264 --> 00:38:32,444
A thin rod in front of a distant wall does not reveal its thickness
through a single depth value.

421
00:38:32,444 --> 00:38:39,164
An excessive sample range can associate unrelated surfaces and
produce a dark halo around the object.

422
00:38:39,167 --> 00:38:45,104
CryEngine 2’s 2007 technical discussion and Unreal documentation
describe screen-based approximations and their limitations.

423
00:38:45,104 --> 00:38:48,304
Actual comparisons need identified versions and settings.

424
00:38:48,467 --> 00:38:55,024
A 2017 Unreal 4.16 demonstration approaches a cube model and
changes the AO Power and Bias controls.

425
00:38:55,124 --> 00:38:57,504
Watch the gaps between cubes and their floor contacts.

426
00:38:57,844 --> 00:39:02,664
Adjusting this contact treatment is different from adding a light
source.

427
00:39:02,967 --> 00:39:10,127
Several controls and the camera change in this sequence, so we will
not attribute every difference to one isolated number.

428
00:39:10,247 --> 00:39:16,687
First observe the actual manipulations and contacts; our separate
diagram isolates sample range and the limits of depth information.

429
00:39:18,967 --> 00:39:21,927
This is not a substitute for direct-light shadows.

430
00:39:21,947 --> 00:39:27,407
Darkening corners does not calculate occlusion for a particular lamp
or colored indirect light reflected from another wall.

431
00:39:27,433 --> 00:39:31,007
Information outside the screen and behind visible surfaces is still
missing.

432
00:39:31,167 --> 00:39:35,547
The same information boundary becomes important for
screen-space reflections in the next episode.

433
00:39:36,287 --> 00:39:42,967
This episode followed assigned brightness, stored surface lighting,
and updated visibility for moving lights.

434
00:39:43,187 --> 00:39:46,527
More lights also changed the ordering of material and lighting work.

435
00:39:46,683 --> 00:39:50,399
To distinguish methods, ask three questions: what is stored,

436
